import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';
---

<BlogLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Restaurant SaaS Global Expansion Insight: an independent journal on restaurant brands, software products, international expansion, and digital operations."
  type="website"
  locale="en"
  alternateUrl="https://blog.foolcar.cc/about/"
  pageType="about"
>
  <article class="about-page">
    <header class="about-hero">
      <div class="about-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">About this journal</p>
        <h1>Turning first-hand questions into notes worth returning to.</h1>
        <p class="about-lede">I work across restaurant software, store operations, and product design. This journal examines the problems that appear when restaurant brands and SaaS products enter new markets.</p>
      </div>
      <figure>
        <img src="/restaurant-counter.jpg" alt="The open kitchen and counter of a restaurant during an ordinary working day" />
        <figcaption>Product decisions eventually return to a real setting: who uses the system, when they use it, and who responds when it fails.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </header>

    <section class="about-section about-note">
      <p class="section-number">Why</p>
      <div>
        <h2>Why I write</h2>
        <p>Conversations about restaurant technology often fall into two extremes. One side discusses markets at a distance; the other compares feature lists. What determines whether a product works is usually more practical: the authority given to local teams, differences in payments and tax, the routines inside a store, and who is accountable when systems meet.</p>
        <p>Writing is not a way to assemble a library of final answers. It helps me separate experience, public evidence, and questions I have not resolved, so each judgement can be examined and revised later.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="section-number">Themes</p>
      <div>
        <h2>What I write about</h2>
        <div class="topic-list">
          <div><strong>Markets and entry</strong><p>Whether a market is worth entering and what the first customers can genuinely validate.</p></div>
          <div><strong>Product and localisation</strong><p>Which differences belong in the product and which requests should not become permanent custom work.</p></div>
          <div><strong>Digital operations</strong><p>How POS, payments, delivery, membership, and reporting return to the daily work of a restaurant.</p></div>
          <div><strong>Teams and collaboration</strong><p>How teams across markets share context, retain autonomy, and maintain common standards.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section about-principles">
      <p class="section-number">Principles</p>
      <div>
        <h2>How I try to write</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><span>Define the problem before discussing a tool or product.</span></li>
          <li><span>Separate first-hand experience, public evidence, and personal inference.</span></li>
          <li><span>Keep uncertainty visible instead of presenting one case as a universal rule.</span></li>
          <li><span>Take a position without turning an article into product promotion.</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <aside class="about-disclosure">
      <strong>A note on perspective</strong>
      <p>Everything here is written in a personal capacity and reflects my judgement at the time. It does not represent any company's official position. When an article involves a partner, product, or commercial interest, I will disclose the relevant context.</p>
    </aside>

    <nav class="about-actions" aria-label="More from the About page">
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/">Back to articles</a>
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/rss.xml">RSS feed</a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</BlogLayout>
